  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on counting how many individual outcomes (unweighted) in each lottery exceed the unweighted average of all outcomes across both lotteries. As calculated, both A and B have one outcome above 2400, so the reason would not favor one over the other. The individual, however, bases their decision on the probability of winning a positive amount — specifically noting that B has a higher chance of yielding a non-zero outcome. This is a different consideration: it involves probability weighting (albeit simply), and focuses on P(win > 0), which is 25% for A (only 6000) and 50% for B (4000 and 2000). The individual explicitly refers to this probability difference as the reason for choosing B. There is no mention of average outcomes, comparisons to a mean, or counting how many outcomes exceed a benchmark. The cognitive process described is probability-focused (chance of winning something) rather than outcome-value comparison against a mean. Therefore, the decision reason is not reflected in the individual’s deliberation. The information used and the justification differ fundamentally from the reason’s logic.",
